<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cerdanya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cerdagne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cerdanya is a high Pyrenean valley of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segre">Segre</a>, divided between Catalonia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Pyrénées-Orientales. It takes its name from the ancient Cerretani, became a Carolingian county and later passed to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659 split it, giving the northern part to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> but leaving the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llivia">Llívia</a> as a Spanish exclave.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cerdanya.jpg" alt="Cerdanya" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MarkCat · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ll%C3%ADvia_flors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
